Standard SSH vs. Direct Command Execution1. Standard SSH Login (ssh bandit18@...)
  -Authentication: You authenticate with your credentials.
1.Default Shell Lookup: sshd checks /etc/passwd on the server to see your assigned default shell (which is set to 2.bash).
3.Launch Bash: sshd launches /bin/bash in login mode.
4.Load Config: bash reads and executes ~/.bashrc.
5.Session Killed: The line added to .bashrc executes exit or logout $\rightarrow$ Your session immediately disconnects.

  -Direct Command Execution (ssh bandit18@... /bin/sh)
1.Authentication: You authenticate with your credentials.
2.Bypass Default Shell: sshd sees an explicit command (/bin/sh) attached to the SSH request and ignores the default shell setting in /etc/passwd.
3.Direct Execution: sshd invokes /bin/sh directly.
4.Skip .bashrc: sh starts up without reading ~/.bashrc $\rightarrow$ You get an active, uninterrupted shell.

